  (18
  ("once what the two young men had found. “Well, boys, we’ve just been scooped,” he told his" 0.11909 0.88151 0.08457 0.10035 0.10035 0.01578 0.00504 0.2995 0.0381 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("colleagues as he hung up the phone." 0.11909 0.40996 0.10103 0.11681 0.11681 0.01578 0.00504 0.2473 0.0852 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  (#("Soon afterward the Astrophysical Journal published two articles: one by Penzias and" 19 41 (pdf-text-emph italic)) 0.13926 0.88139 0.13395 0.14973 0.14973 0.01578 0.00504 0.2385 0.0415 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("Wilson describing their experience with the hiss, the other by Dicke’s team explaining its" 0.11909 0.8814 0.15034 0.16612 0.16612 0.01578 0.00504 0.2763 0.0582 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("nature. Although Penzias and Wilson had not been looking for cosmic background radiation," 0.11909 0.88149 0.16673 0.18251 0.18251 0.01578 0.00504 0.2774 0.0554 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("didn’t know what it was when they had found it, and hadn’t described or interpreted its" 0.11909 0.88135 0.18312 0.1989 0.1989 0.01578 0.00504 0.2393 0.0481 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("character in any paper, they received the 1978 Nobel Prize in physics. The Princeton" 0.11909 0.88134 0.19951 0.21529 0.21529 0.01578 0.00504 0.2200 0.0740 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  (#("researchers got only sympathy. According to Dennis Overbye in Lonely Hearts of the Cosmos" 62 89 (pdf-text-emph italic)) 0.11909 0.88147 0.2159 0.23168 0.23168 0.01578 0.00504 0.3017 0.0907 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  (", neither Penzias nor Wilson altogether understood the significance of what they had found" 0.11909 0.88151 0.23229 0.24807 0.24807 0.01578 0.00504 0.2531 0.0050 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  (#("until they read about it in the New York Times ." 32 47 (pdf-text-emph italic)) 0.11909 0.49946 0.24875 0.26453 0.26453 0.01578 0.00504 0.3329 0.0370 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("Incidentally, disturbance from cosmic background radiation is something we have all" 0.13926 0.8814 0.28167 0.29745 0.29745 0.01578 0.00504 0.2727 0.1014 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("experienced. Tune your television to any channel it doesn’t receive, and about 1 percent of the" 0.11909 0.88166 0.29806 0.31384 0.31384 0.01578 0.00504 0.2598 0.1025 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("dancing static you see is accounted for by this ancient remnant of the Big Bang. The next time" 0.11909 0.88148 0.31445 0.33023 0.33023 0.01578 0.00504 0.3055 0.0639 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("you complain that there is nothing on, remember that you can always watch the birth of the" 0.11909 0.88144 0.33084 0.34662 0.34662 0.01578 0.00504 0.2802 0.0303 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("universe." 0.11909 0.19259 0.3473 0.36308 0.36308 0.01578 0.00504 0.2265 0.0735 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("Although everyone calls it the Big Bang, many books caution us not to think of it as an" 0.13926 0.88151 0.38029 0.39607 0.39607 0.01578 0.00504 0.2614 0.0763 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("explosion in the conventional sense. It was, rather, a vast, sudden expansion on a whopping" 0.11909 0.8815 0.39668 0.41246 0.41246 0.01578 0.00504 0.2384 0.0785 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("scale. So what caused it?" 0.11909 0.31997 0.41314 0.42892 0.42892 0.01578 0.00504 0.2990 0.0454 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("One notion is that perhaps the singularity was the relic of an earlier, collapsed universe—" 0.13419 0.88148 0.44607 0.46184 0.46184 0.01578 0.00504 0.2501 0.0336 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("that we’re just one of an eternal cycle of expanding and collapsing universes, like the bladder" 0.11909 0.88146 0.46245 0.47823 0.47823 0.01578 0.00504 0.2508 0.0303 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("on an oxygen machine. Others attribute the Big Bang to what they call “a false vacuum” or “a" 0.11909 0.88161 0.47884 0.49462 0.49462 0.01578 0.00504 0.3078 0.0202 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("scalar field” or “vacuum energy”—some quality or thing, at any rate, that introduced a" 0.11909 0.88142 0.49523 0.51101 0.51101 0.01578 0.00504 0.2659 0.0471 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("measure of instability into the nothingness that was. It seems impossible that you could get" 0.11909 0.88153 0.51162 0.5274 0.5274 0.01578 0.00504 0.2741 0.0671 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("something from nothing, but the fact that once there was nothing and now there is a universe" 0.11909 0.88139 0.52801 0.54379 0.54379 0.01578 0.00504 0.2841 0.0840 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("is evident proof that you can. It may be that our universe is merely part of many larger" 0.11909 0.88154 0.5444 0.56018 0.56018 0.01578 0.00504 0.2369 0.0135 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("universes, some in different dimensions, and that Big Bangs are going on all the time all over" 0.11909 0.88163 0.56079 0.57657 0.57657 0.01578 0.00504 0.3038 0.0813 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("the place. Or it may be that space and time had some other forms altogether before the Big" 0.11909 0.88157 0.57718 0.59296 0.59296 0.01578 0.00504 0.2803 0.0247 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("Bang—forms too alien for us to imagine—and that the Big Bang represents some sort of" 0.11909 0.88135 0.59357 0.60935 0.60935 0.01578 0.00504 0.3082 0.1099 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("transition phase, where the universe went from a form we can’t understand to one we almost" 0.11909 0.88138 0.60996 0.62574 0.62574 0.01578 0.00504 0.2999 0.0762 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("can. “These are very close to religious questions,” Dr. Andrei Linde, a cosmologist at" 0.11909 0.8815 0.62635 0.64213 0.64213 0.01578 0.00504 0.2538 0.0330 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  (#("Stanford, told the New York Times in 2001." 19 34 (pdf-text-emph italic)) 0.11909 0.46812 0.64281 0.65859 0.65859 0.01578 0.00504 0.3229 0.0746 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("The Big Bang theory isn’t about the bang itself but about what happened after the bang." 0.13926 0.88163 0.67573 0.69151 0.69151 0.01578 0.00504 0.2479 0.0314 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("Not long after, mind you. By doing a lot of math and watching carefully what goes on in" 0.11909 0.88152 0.69212 0.7079 0.7079 0.01578 0.00504 0.2651 0.0303 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("particle accelerators, scientists believe they can look back to 10^{-43}seconds after the moment of" 0.11909 0.88155 0.70621 0.72429 0.72429 0.01578 0.00504 0.2766 0.0605 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("creation, when the universe was still so small that you would have needed a microscope to" 0.11909 0.88152 0.7249 0.74068 0.74068 0.01578 0.00504 0.2620 0.0700 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("find it. We mustn’t swoon over every extraordinary number that comes before us, but it is" 0.11909 0.88152 0.74129 0.75707 0.75707 0.01578 0.00504 0.2880 0.0325 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("perhaps worth latching on to one from time to time just to be reminded of their ungraspable" 0.11909 0.88154 0.75768 0.77346 0.77346 0.01578 0.00504 0.2895 0.0628 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("and amazing breadth. Thus 10^{-43}is 0.0000000000000000000000000000000000000000001, or" 0.11909 0.88145 0.77177 0.78985 0.78985 0.01578 0.00504 0.2036 0.0291 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("one 10 million trillion trillion trillionths of a second.^{*}" 0.11909 0.54882 0.78823 0.80631 0.80631 0.01578 0.00504 0.3151 0.0291 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("                                                 " nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil nil)
  ("*A word on scientific notation: Since very large numbers are cumbersome to write and nearly impossible to read, scientists" 0.11909 0.88148 0.84665 0.85848 0.85848 0.01183 0.00378 0.2917 0.0185 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0107 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("use a shorthand involving powers (or multiples) of ten in which, for instance, 10,000,000,000 is written 1010 and 6,500,000" 0.11909 0.88155 0.85898 0.87081 0.87081 0.01183 0.00378 0.2801 0.0202 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0107 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("becomes 6.5 x 106. The principle is based very simply on multiples of ten: 10 x 10 (or 100) becomes 102; 10 x 10 x 10 (or" 0.11909 0.88126 0.87124 0.88307 0.88307 0.01183 0.00378 0.2773 0.0529 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0107 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("1,000) is 103; and so on, obviously and indefinitely. The little superscript number signifies the number of zeroes following" 0.11909 0.88144 0.88356 0.89539 0.89539 0.01183 0.00378 0.2668 0.0391 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0107 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("the larger principal number. Negative notations provide latter in print (especially essentially a mirror image, with the" 0.11909 0.88157 0.89582 0.90765 0.90765 0.01183 0.00378 0.2697 0.0185 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0107 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("superscript number indicating the number of spaces to the right of the decimal point (so 10-4 means 0.0001). Though I salute" 0.11909 0.88137 0.90814 0.91998 0.91998 0.01183 0.00378 0.2942 0.0665 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0107 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("the principle, it remains an amazement to me that anyone seeing \"1.4 x 109 km3’ would see at once that that signifies 1.4" 0.11909 0.88148 0.9204 0.93224 0.93224 0.01183 0.00378 0.2793 0.0185 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0107 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  (#("Bill Bryson" 0 11 (pdf-text-emph bold-italic)) 0.0605 0.14922 0.97282 0.98938 0.98938 0.01656 0.00467 0.3635 0.0261 "Arial,BoldItalic" 0.0119 t t 0 "Arial,BoldItalic" t)
  (#("Short History of Nearly Everything" 0 34 (pdf-text-emph bold-italic)) 0.36103 0.6356 0.97282 0.98938 0.98938 0.01656 0.00467 0.3145 0.0439 "Arial,BoldItalic" 0.0119 t t 0 "Arial,BoldItalic" t)
  (#("Page 4" 0 6 (pdf-text-emph bold-italic)) 0.88195 0.93613 0.97282 0.98938 0.98938 0.01656 0.00467 0.2516 0.0402 "Arial,BoldItalic" 0.0119 t t 0 "Arial,BoldItalic" t))
  (19
  ("Most of what we know, or believe we know, about the early moments of the universe is" 0.13926 0.88151 0.08457 0.10035 0.10035 0.01578 0.00504 0.3107 0.0415 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("thanks to an idea called inflation theory first propounded in 1979 by a junior particle" 0.11909 0.88151 0.10096 0.11674 0.11674 0.01578 0.00504 0.2199 0.0527 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("physicist, then at Stanford, now at MIT, named Alan Guth. He was thirty-two years old and," 0.11909 0.88139 0.11735 0.13313 0.13313 0.01578 0.00504 0.3328 0.0768 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("by his own admission, had never done anything much before. He would probably never have" 0.11909 0.88149 0.13374 0.14952 0.14952 0.01578 0.00504 0.2708 0.0202 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("had his great theory except that he happened to attend a lecture on the Big Bang given by" 0.11909 0.88142 0.15013 0.1659 0.1659 0.01578 0.00504 0.2041 0.0291 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("none other than Robert Dicke. The lecture inspired Guth to take an interest in cosmology, and" 0.11909 0.88141 0.16652 0.18229 0.18229 0.01578 0.00504 0.2960 0.0392 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("in particular in the birth of the universe." 0.11909 0.44063 0.18298 0.19875 0.19875 0.01578 0.00504 0.2762 0.0157 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("The eventual result was the inflation theory, which holds that a fraction of a moment after" 0.13926 0.88145 0.2159 0.23168 0.23168 0.01578 0.00504 0.2966 0.0314 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("the dawn of creation, the universe underwent a sudden dramatic expansion. It inflated—in" 0.11909 0.88135 0.23229 0.24807 0.24807 0.01578 0.00504 0.2743 0.0247 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("effect ran away with itself, doubling in size every 10^{-34}seconds. The whole episode may have" 0.11909 0.88133 0.24638 0.26446 0.26446 0.01578 0.00504 0.2826 0.0459 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("lasted no more than 10^{-30}seconds—that’s one million million million million millionths of a" 0.11909 0.88133 0.26277 0.28085 0.28085 0.01578 0.00504 0.3503 0.0471 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("second—but it changed the universe from something you could hold in your hand to" 0.11909 0.8814 0.28146 0.29723 0.29723 0.01578 0.00504 0.2485 0.1020 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("something at least 10,000,000,000,000,000,000,000,000 times bigger. Inflation theory" 0.11909 0.88151 0.29785 0.31362 0.31362 0.01578 0.00504 0.3097 0.0839 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
  ("explains the ripples and eddies that make our universe possible. Without it, there would be no" 0.11909 0.88148 0.31424 0.33001 0.33001 0.01578 0.00504 0.3082 0.0673 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" 0.0143 nil nil 0 "KOPLLM+TimesNewRoman" nil)
